"## Summary\n\nUsage follow-up 9: devstack 33's shared node write replaces usage's own retry loop in `nodeMutation`.\n- **Session:** launched 21:17 EST on 2026-11-03, and delivered at 21:47. One review fix was delivered at 23:13; its gates waited for admission while two assembled runs were active.\n- **Model:** agy `gemini-4-argon-high`.\n- **Size:** 4 commits and 11 owned files, +1,061/\u221264.\n- **Checks:**\n  - merge-tree clean on current `main`; prepush-scan clean; owned-check passes.\n  - **The orchestrator reran these** on a scratch tree: current `main` (`41060705`, after #448) with this branch merged in. All pass:\n    - `make -f mk/at-usage.mk at-usage-lint`, `at-usage-test` and `at-usage-build`, and the four new offline tests with `-tags e2e`;\n    - `make -f mk/e2e.mk e2e-lint` and `go vet -tags e2e ./tests/e2e/...`;\n    - `go test ./tests/handover/...` and the committed-evidence tests;\n    - `make go-lint`, gofmt and `git diff --check`.\n- **Scope:** offline. No stack, no Docker object and no reservation.\n\n**The orchestrator's reading.**\n- **Devstack 32's audit routed one helper to this lane:** `nodeMutation`, which carries all 16 node-write call sites in usage's tests.\n  - **Before:** up to four rounds of a read and a write on any 412. Each round made a new idempotency key, so a retry could be a second command.\n  - **Now:** every call goes through `labstack`'s `Stack.WriteNode` (#441). The retry is the contract's: the same key and the same body, only after exactly `RESOURCE_VERSION_CONFLICT`, at most three writes in ten seconds.\n- **Each command has an exact read-back predicate:**\n  - the capacity PATCH: the capacity fields it sets;\n  - the publication PUT: the exact published groups, or the disabled intent with its groups;\n  - the revoke: `NodeRevoked`;\n  - the DELETE: `lifecycle_state` `retired`.\n  - A command whose body the helper cannot judge is refused before any write, and so is one whose initial read failed.\n- **Each command accepts only the contract's statuses:** 200 or 202 for the PATCH, and 202 for the publication PUT, the revoke and the DELETE. An already-intended read-back after a version conflict also counts, as the helper defines it.\n- **Receipts are embedded** in the records of the checks that use them: every AT14\u2013AT19 record, D-067's bring-up, AT19's node-2 setup and re-enrolment, and AT20's capacity, publication, revoke and D-130 republish.\n- **Every caller's observed-state checks are unchanged.** When a revoke's read-back is already revoked after a conflict, the caller finds the real `node.revoke` operation in `operations`. It does not invent one.\n- **An untagged AST audit** rejects any bypass of the helper. Injected regressions prove that it fails on one. `at-usage-test` now also runs the `e2e`-tagged offline tests.\n- **One review fix** (`f2fb3678`). The first delivery passed 200 and 202 as success for every command. Seven publication and revoke checks therefore accepted 200 where they had accepted exactly 202, which the contract defines. The fix restores 202 for those commands, and its offline tests prove that a 200 is refused for them while the attempt is still recorded.\n- **No live claim.** The committed run records stay as they are. The live proof is the next authorized assembled run.\n- **No requests.** Devstack 33's hand-off to this lane is answered.\n"
